Date: Fri, 5 Feb 93 10:11:03 PST
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: ECON: Zero-sum games

My mail system was apparently down last night so I may have missed some
discussion on the zero-sum game topic since my last post.  I do have
some more thoughts on it, though.

Robin would like to see betting markets available where people could bet
on a wide range of future events.  He has described these "idea futures"
in many places, including Extropy magazine last year, and they do seem
like they would provide a lot of useful information.

I gather from this discussion, that Robin thinks it would be possible
and even necessayr for "investors" in these markets to make net profits
that are commensurate with their risks.  This is why the discussion is
relevant - if pure betting markets do not allow for net profits (as I
have been arguing) then this will presumably be impossible.

Robin's markets would support bets on such things as whether nanotech
will be possible by the year 2010.  (Obviously the contracts would go into
a lot of detail to define the terms and conditions, as Robin has discussed
at length.)  Now, my question is, if Robin is running a market which
allows such bets, how will he offer a profit to the bettors?  Isn't it
true that current market makers, such as bookies and Las Vegas casinos,
actually do the opposite - that is, the total payout is less than what
is paid in?

The only thing I could see is if he takes the money which he requires
the bettors to pay "up front", holds it, and invests it.  This way it
will earn income over the period of time until the bet is concluded,
and he can use that income to pay the bettors a profit.

This is roughly similar to Robin's suggestion that bettors on football
games could bet treasury bonds rather than cash, so that a profit can
be earned.

The problem with this that I see is whether the degree of profit is
appropriate for the risk.  It seems to me that the market maker is not
going to be able to get a very high return if he is a prudent
businessman.  It would be unwise to put that money into a risky
investment since he _knows_ that he is going to have to pay it back, in
full, plus interest.  So I think he is going to have to put it into a
relatively riskless investment, which means he is only going to be able
to offer the bettors a rate of return appropriate for a riskless
investment (less his own profits).

Now, this raises the question of how you should measure the risk of a
betting transaction.  My initial reaction was that the risk is quite
high, since there is close to a 50% chance you will lose all your money
(with a corresponding 50% chance that you will double your money).  A
stock which had this property would be viewed as very risky.

But with more thought, I'm not sure this is the best way to look at it.
Stock riskiness is traditionally measured in terms of volatility of the
stock's price compared to that of the market as a whole.  This is called
the "beta" of the stock, as I understand it.  The reason for this definition
is that you can diversify your investments across a whole lot of stocks
with similar betas, and now you are protected against the vagaries of price
movements of individual stocks.  You are still vulnerable to market-wide
price shifts, and the beta measures how vulnerable you are.  Your return
should then be determined by the beta of your investment basket.

Applying a similar analysis to betting, one could presumably diversify
and bet on a whole lot of different issues.  If you figured that you
were right about 50% of the time, the overall risk would be reduced
considerably because you'd lose some and win on others.  If you bet on
enough things, the risk would perhaps approach zero?  And in that
sense, perhaps the zero-risk rate of return which Robin could offer his
bettors is actually the right rate.  In that sense, betting is _not_ a
risky activity at all.

Hal

