> From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
> 
> I claim, however, that this standard line is just wrong, that stock
> markets are no less zero-sum than futures, or than many other kinds of
> betting markets I would like to introduce.  I challenge anyone to
> offer a (relevant) *precise* definition of zero-sum which shows
> otherwise.  (It is certainly not true according to the standard
> definition of a zero-sum game.)  In fact, I offer a $100 donation to
> ExI in the name of whoever can do so.

John buys one share of XYZ corp from James with $100. Later that week,
one share of XYZ corp is valued at $200.

James assets: starts with share worth $100, ends with $100
              net change: $0
Johns assets: starts with $100, ends with share worth $200
              net change: +$100
total net change for system: +$100

James writes John a futures contract in XYZ corp at $100. Later that week, 
one share of XYZ corp is valued at $200. The contract is settled that day. 
John pays James $100. James buys a share of XYZ at $200 and gives it
to John. We assume that James and John each start with a $100 bill in pocket.

James assets: starts with $100, ends having $0
              net change: -$100
Johns assets: starts with $100, ends with share worth $200
              net change: +$100
total net change for system: $0

Here endeth the lesson.

