Perry E. Metzger writes:
>John buys one share of XYZ corp from James with $100. Later that week,
>one share of XYZ corp is valued at $200.
>
>James assets: starts with share worth $100, ends with $100
>              net change: $0
>Johns assets: starts with $100, ends with share worth $200
>              net change: +$100
>total net change for system: +$100
>
>James writes John a futures contract in XYZ corp at $100. Later that week, 
>one share of XYZ corp is valued at $200. The contract is settled that day. 
>John pays James $100. James buys a share of XYZ at $200 and gives it
>to John. We assume that James and John each start with a $100 bill in pocket.
>
>James assets: starts with $100, ends having $0
>              net change: -$100
>Johns assets: starts with $100, ends with share worth $200
>              net change: +$100
>total net change for system: $0

You are here bundling transactions and investment strategies, and
trying to infer a relative property of the transactions (stock vs.
futures) from a property of the investment strategy in your example.
However, these same transactions could just as easily be bundled with
different investment strategies, which undercuts your inference.

Transaction 1: John buys one share of XYZ corp from James with $100 now.
Transaction 2: James now writes John a futures contract in XYZ corp at
  $100, to be settled in one week.

Investment Strategy 1: John holds stock, James holds cash.
Investment Strategy 2: Both hold cash. 

To switch the transactions and investment strategies, imagine John was
short the stock and had that much more cash before transaction 1, and
that John held the stock instead of cash before transaction 2.  After
this switch, the futures example now has the positive net change.

The return on an investment strategy held by a person who makes a
transaction is in general not an intrinsic property of that
transaction.  The transaction only constrains *changes* in the
investment strategy.

Robin Hanson


