Even though I don't subscribe to much of the dream of eliminating
government, it's clearly possible to privatize various government
elements.  In the long run, this could reduce the size of the existing
governments enormously.  For instance, the government welfare system
could simply be eliminated, by law.  PPLs could be created in the
current society by simply writing the needed contracts.  Hell, with
proper political support, the United States government could be
abolished; all you have to do is amend the Constitution.

The practical problem is whether enough of the voters would consider
such privatizations to be in their interests to pass such laws.  On
the whole, it appears they don't.  Given that most libertarians are
well-employed professional workers (and male), it may be that
libertarian ideals only strongly appeal to a small class of people,
mostly those who perceive that they would benefit immediately from
their implementation.

